<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="sulfite oxidation IV (sulfite oxidase)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5326" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Sulfite in excess will cause damage to the organism, by breaking disulfide bridges (sulfitolysis), forming metabolic inhibitors such as hydroxysulfonates and interfere with the availability of essential cations required for nutrition |CITS: [Heber98]|. Sulfite oxidation is different in plants and animals. In vertebrates the role of sulfite oxidation lays in the detoxification of sulfite derived from the decomposition of sulfur-containing amino acids. In addition, sulfite is formed from sulfur dioxide, an environmental pollutant, and is provided exogenously, either as a constituent of certain fruits, or as a preservative in dried fruits, vegetables and wine |CITS: [8586770]|. In plants, sulfite is produced as an intermediate during the assimilation of sulfur, by reducing sulfate via sulfite to sulfide to form cysteine. Both animals and plants possess |FRAME: EC-1.8.3.1|, a molybdenum-containing enzyme that converts sulfite to sulate using oxygen as the electron donor. One major point of difference is the location of the enzyme, which is found in vertebrates in the mitochondria, but is localized to the peroxisome in plants |CITS: [15653809]|. In animals the enzyme is is located in the mitochondrial intermembrane space and is active mostly in the liver, where it catalyzes the terminal reaction in the oxidative degradation of the sulfur-containing amino acids cysteine and methionine (see |FRAME: PWY-5328|). The enzyme has been isolated from different sources and exists as a homodimer of molecular weight in the range 101-110 kDa. In animals (but not plants) the enzyme also contains a heme group. Each subunit can be proteolytically cleaved into two domains |CITS:[14956]|, the smaller of which contains a &lt;i&gt;b&lt;sub&gt;5&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/i&gt; cytochrome, while the larger harbors the molybdenum cofactor |CITS: [9242907]|. Sulfite oxidase deficiency is a lethal genetic disease in animals. It can be caused by defects in the sulfite oxidase gene, or in genes involved in molybdenum cofactor biosynthesis |CITS: [16048997]|. In plants sulfite oxidation constitutes a converse pathway to sulfate assimilation that occurrs in chloroplasts. The peroxisomal location of sulfite detoxification solves the problem of having two counteracting pathways in the same cell compartment |CITS: [16307306]|. In contrast to its animal counterpart the plant sulfite oxidase lacks the heme domain, hence being the only known Mo enzyme that possesses no other redox-active centers |CITS: [11598126][16407262]|. While sulfite produced during the process of sulfate assimilation is converted into sulfide rapidly, sulfite oxidase has been described as a &apos;safety valve&apos; to detoxify increased levels of sulfite |CITS: [16307306]|, which would be harmful to plants. Both enzymatic and non-enzymatic steps are involved in the sulfite oxidation in plants under conditions of high sulfite concentration, as the catalase in the peroxisomes is inhibited by sulfite, which would result in excess hydrogen peroxide, which oxidizes sulfite non-enzymatically |CITS: [16407262]|.</Description>
